//! # C17 Statement Parser (phase 23b Phase 4)
//!
//! Public entry point for the C statement parser. The implementation lives
//! alongside declaration and expression parsing in `parser.tml` because
//! C's statement grammar is mutually recursive with declarations (C99
//! mixed decls+stmts inside a block, C99 for-init declarations) and with
//! expressions (condition, return value, case value, etc.). Splitting the
//! implementation into a separate file would introduce a circular `use`
//! import that TML doesn't support; this module provides a curated view
//! of the statement-parsing surface.
//!
//! ## Entry points
//! - `cp_parse_compound_stmt(parser, pos) -> Outcome[CParsedStmt, CParseError]` —
//!   a `{ ... }` block. Each item inside is either a declaration (C99
//!   mixed decls) or a statement.
//! - `cp_parse_stmt(parser, pos) -> Outcome[CParsedStmt, CParseError]` —
//!   a single statement (dispatches to the right sub-parser).
//!
//! ## Statement coverage
//!
//! | Form                                  | AST variant              | Status |
//! |---------------------------------------|--------------------------|--------|
//! | `;`                                   | `CStmt::Empty`           | ✓      |
//! | `expr ;`                              | `CStmt::Expr`            | ✓      |
//! | `{ items... }`                        | `CStmt::Compound`        | ✓      |
//! | `if (e) s [else s]`                   | `CStmt::If`              | ✓      |
//! | `for (init; cond; upd) body`          | `CStmt::For`             | ✓      |
//! | `while (cond) body`                   | `CStmt::While`           | ✓      |
//! | `do body while (cond);`               | `CStmt::DoWhile`         | ✓      |
//! | `switch (e) body`                     | `CStmt::Switch`          | ✓      |
//! | `case e : s`                          | `CStmt::Case`            | ✓      |
//! | `default : s`                         | `CStmt::Default`         | ✓      |
//! | `break ;` / `continue ;`              | `CStmt::Break`/`Continue`| ✓      |
//! | `return [e] ;`                        | `CStmt::Return`          | ✓      |
//! | `goto label ;`                        | `CStmt::Goto`            | ✓      |
//! | `label : s`                           | `CStmt::Label`           | ✓      |
//! | `_Static_assert(e, "msg") ;`          | `CStmt::StaticAssert`    | ✓      |
//!
//! ## Mixed declarations and statements (C99)
//!
//! `cp_parse_compound_stmt` detects declarations by `cp_at_decl_specifier`
//! — when the current token starts a declaration-specifier list it routes
//! to `cp_parse_top_decl` and wraps every resulting `Heap[CDecl]` in a
//! `CBlockItem::DeclItem`. Otherwise it parses a statement via
//! `cp_parse_stmt`. The `label_ahead` helper disambiguates `typedef-name :`
//! (not a valid label — typedef names are expression identifiers) from
//! `ident :` (a C label) by looking one token ahead.
//!
//! ## For-init declarations (C99)
//!
//! The `for` header's first slot accepts either an expression-statement
//! or a declaration. When `cp_at_decl_specifier` returns true we route to
//! `cp_parse_top_decl` and stash the first resulting decl in the
//! `CStmt::For` init slot — MIR lowering (phase 23b Phase 6) expands
//! multiple declarators into a compound block item.
//!
//! ## Attribute and alignment extensions
//!
//! - `__attribute__((...))` — GCC extension used in system headers. The
//!   preprocessor doesn't strip it; we skip the entire `__attribute__((...))`
//!   token sequence at declaration-specifier time (the parser's tokenizer
//!   never sees it because the C preprocessor from phase23a already
//!   consumed it — if we encounter it at statement position we treat it
//!   as a no-op ahead of the next statement).
//! - `_Alignas(N)` — already handled by `cp_parse_specifiers` (item 2.1)
//!   via specifier kind 6; the type checker applies it to the aggregate
//!   layout.

use compiler::cc::parser::{
    cp_parse_compound_stmt,
    cp_parse_stmt,
    CParsedStmt,
    CParseError
}

pub type ReExports {
    _placeholder: I64
}
